  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nMainly mid and high clouds will continue to stream across the \nregion today ahead of an approaching shortwave trough. This\ntrough is progged to weaken through the day, with decreasing\nascent as it moves eastward. Expect some chance for light rain\nor sprinkles this morning mainly across Ohio, especially near \nand west of I-77. Dry low level air in place should limit the \npotential for measurable rainfall. The weakening trough should \nexit the region this afternoon. A weak surface warm front is \nprogged to lift NE across the area tonight, though a lack of \nmoisture and ascent should limit rain chances to slight, mainly \nacross OH and NW PA. \n\nRidging, ahead of a deepening central CONUS trough, is expected\nto build across the Upper Ohio Valley region Tuesday into early\nWednesday. Rising 500 mb heights and southerly flow should\nresult in high temperatures from 15 to 20 degrees above average\nboth days. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe central CONUS trough is expected to advance eastward,\napproaching the Upper Ohio Valley region late Wednesday. Rain\nchances increase later in the day, peaking Wednesday night into\nThursday as the trough crosses, along with a surface occluded\nfront. Limited elevated instability is progged with the surface\nocclusion, with a rumble of thunder possible. \n\nAn unsettled pattern is then expected from late week into the\nweekend, as model ensembles indicate a persistent broad upper\ntrough across the Great Lakes-NE CONUS region. Shortwaves\nrotating through the trough will keep periodic rain chances in\nthe forecast into at least the first part of the weekend.",
